{"_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Constants in C#"}, {"type": "position", "content": 8}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 3}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear/use when talking about C# constants."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Talk about principles, concepts, characteristics of general-purpose computer programming language in spoken and written situations."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob08re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "May and Can to Talk About Possibility"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "To express possibility, we can use the modal verbs <strong>can</strong> and <strong>may</strong>; they have different degree of certainty.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "We use <strong>can</strong> to talk about a general possibility; something that is likely to happen frequently.   </br></br>   Form: Subject + <strong>can + base form of the verb</strong> + complement  </br></br>   Let's understand a little bit more by checking some examples:  </br></br><i>Programming <strong>can be</strong> very hard at the beginning. </i> (There is the possibility of finding programming hard at the beginning, because several other people have experienced it that way.   </br></br>   <i>You <strong>can fail</strong> the test on C# if you don't study hard enough. </i> (Very often, when people don't study, they fail their tests).   </br></br> We use <strong>may</strong> to express the possibility of something to happen, either in the present or in the future.     </br> </br> Form: Subject + <strong>may + verb</strong> complement   </br></br>   Let's understand a little bit more by checking some examples:   </br></br>   <i>It <strong>may</strong> rain tomorrow!</i> (There exists the possibility of a rain the day after. </strong>   </br></br>  <i> Carla brought her car today; she <strong>may give</strong> you a ride. </i>  (There exists the possibility of Carla taking the person to another place)."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Constants of C# are fixed values that the programmer may not alter during its execution"}, {"type": "example", "content": "So, we may want to say that integer literals can be a decimal or a hexadecimal constant."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Maybe we can put in a different slide with bright colors."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Going to Have to for What Is Necessary"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "We use <strong> going to have to</strong> to talk about thinks that become, at certain point, obligations."}, {"type": "text", "content": "We use <strong> going to have to</strong> with two purposes mainly.  </br></br> Structure: Subject + verb to be + <strong>going to have to</strong> + infinitive + complement.  </br></br>To say that someone should do something: </br></br>Example: <i> You're <strong>going to have to</strong> code a new program tomorrow.</i> </br></br> You can also use this structure to talk about things you didn't want to do, but now they have become an obligation. </br></br>Example: <i>I'm <strong>going to have to</strong> start the code programming again; there's a mistake from the very core of it.</i> </br></br>This structure is not so common in written English, but it is so in colloquial English. However, native speakers always pronounce <strong>going to</strong> in the contracted form <strong>gonna</strong>."}, {"type": "example", "content": "You have got to talk about the prefix and the suffix as well. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "We are going to have to include that it has an integer part, a decimal point, a fractional part, and an exponent part. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "break down"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to divide into sections"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Then we're going to have to break down the different types of constants."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob08aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "should suffice "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "should be enough"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Just a brief explanation should suffice."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob08aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "hand out "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to give something to a group, such as information or material"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(phrasal verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I believe that we should hand out a photocopy with all the different combinations of characters. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob08aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "highlight "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to draw attention"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I believe that we should hand out a photocopy with all the different combinations of characters. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob08aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "single quotes "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "one pair of single quotation marks"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: However, during the presentation, we should highlight that characters are enclosed in single quotes. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob08aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Do further research about C# constants and literals and the differences between C and C++. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Write down those differences individually by using can and may to say what things are possible to be done with them. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share your findings with your partners."}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I do research on specific topics. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I write differences between C, C++ and C# while making use of grammar topics such as can and may for possibility.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I share my findings to my class."}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "type": "welcome", "id": 0, "contents": [{}]}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "0", "image": "enpe7n5le03ob08re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<strong>Wolfgang</strong>: Hey, Baxter! Come here a second. This is Ruth. She's a programmer from our Vermont branch. You two are going to have to create a mini presentation on the constants of C# for the next training session; don't worry, you'll be paid overtime.</br>\n<strong>Ruth</strong>: Hi, Baxter.</br>\n<strong>Baxter</strong>: Hi, Ruth.</br>\n<strong>Wolfgang</strong>: I'll leave you two to it. Let me know if you need anything.</br>\n<strong>Ruth & Baxter</strong>: Yes, sir!"}, {"time": "28000", "image": "enpe7n5le03ob08re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<strong>Ruth</strong>: Ok, so this should be pretty simple. In the first slide, we should explain that constants are sometimes also called literals and that they are fixed values that the programmer may not alter during its execution.</br>\n<strong>Baxter</strong>: Correct. Then we're going to have to break down the different types of constants: Integer literals, floating-point literals, character constants, and string literals; just a brief explanation should suffice.</br>\n<strong>Ruth</strong>: Right. So, we may want to say that integer literals can be a decimal or a hexadecimal constant.</br>\n<strong>Baxter</strong>: You have got to talk about the prefix and the suffix as well. Maybe we can put in a different slide with bright colors:\n\"Prefix specifies the base: 0x or 0X for hexadecimal. No prefix id for decimal.\" In the second slide, we can project, \"Suffix: a combination of U (unsigned) and L (long); the suffix is not case sensitive.\""}, {"time": "96500", "image": "enpe7n5le03ob08re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<strong>Ruth</strong>: Great idea! When we talk about floating-point, we are going to have to include that it has an integer part, a decimal point, a fractional part, and an exponent part. </br>\n<strong>Baxter</strong>: I believe that we should hand out a photocopy with all the different combinations of characters. However, during the presentation, we should highlight that characters are enclosed in single quotes. We must name at least three categories: universal character, escape sequence, and plain character. They'll have the explanation on paper."}], "coverimg": "enpe7n5le03ob08re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n5le03ob08re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about the concept of constants in C#. "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can list different kinds of constants in C#. "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about characteristics of different 